# What is allowed to reach the Docker daemon when the brain image is built.
#
# The spec's rule is absolute: private data "cannot enter an image layer or
# build context". A conventional ignore file — a list of things to leave out —
# cannot deliver that, because it is only ever as complete as the last person to
# think about it, and the cost of one forgotten line is the owner's entire brain
# uploaded to a daemon and cached there. So this file is written the other way
# round: DENY EVERYTHING, then re-admit the handful of paths the engine is made
# of. A new directory in this repo is excluded by default, on the day it is
# created, by someone who has never read this file.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# READ THIS BEFORE WIRING THE BUILD.
#
# THIS FILE LIVES AT THE REPOSITORY ROOT, and it has to.
#
# BuildKit does not look for an ignore file next to the Dockerfile. It looks for
#   1. <dockerfile-path>.dockerignore   e.g. deploy/Dockerfile.dockerignore
#   2. <context-root>/.dockerignore     e.g. ./.dockerignore
# and the build context for this image MUST be the repository root, because the
# engine lives in bin/ and setup/. It was originally written as
# deploy/.dockerignore, next to the Dockerfile, where anyone editing the build
# would look for it — and where Docker never reads it. A build with no effective
# ignore file uploads knowledge/ and .git/ to the daemon, which is the exact
# thing the spec forbids, and it does so silently.
#
# The root spelling is chosen over deploy/Dockerfile.dockerignore because it is
# the one name BOTH builders honour: candidate 2 for BuildKit, and the only
# candidate the classic builder has ever looked at. One file, no second copy to
# drift out of step with this one.
#
# Every pattern below is written RELATIVE TO THE REPOSITORY ROOT.
#
# This file is defence in depth, not the guarantee. deploy/Dockerfile copies an
# explicit allowlist of paths and then ASSERTS, in a build step that fails the
# build, that no knowledge/, no .git/ and no secret-shaped file made it into the
# image. If this file is ever mis-wired, the build breaks loudly instead of
# publishing an image full of private notes.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Deny everything. In .dockerignore, unlike a shell, `*` matches dotfiles too,
# so this covers .git, .env and every editor and agent directory.
*

# The engine, and only the engine.
!bin
!bin/**
!setup
!setup/**
!.githooks
!.githooks/**
!AGENTS.md
!CLAUDE.md
!README.md

# The entrypoint, which is the one thing under deploy/ that is copied INTO the
# image. The Dockerfile itself does not need to be in the context.
!deploy/entrypoint.sh

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Re-denied, after the allowances above. These paths live INSIDE directories
# that were just re-admitted wholesale, so `*` no longer protects them.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Bytecode from the host. The image compiles its own (see the Dockerfile);
# importing a .pyc built by another interpreter version is at best dead weight
# and at worst a stale copy of code that has since changed.
**/__pycache__
**/*.pyc

# The AUTHOR's age recipient. It is a public key, so this is not a leak — but it
# is the wrong key for this deployment. The VPS encrypts its disaster-recovery
# archives to the recipient supplied in /etc/brain, and an image that quietly
# carried the laptop's recipient would produce backups the server can create and
# the operator cannot explain.
setup/vault-recipient.txt

# Machine-local wiring: absolute paths from whichever checkout ran `brain init`.
# Both are gitignored, so they only exist in a working tree — which is exactly
# the tree a build runs from.
.mcp.json
setup/skills/brain/SKILL.md

# Belt and braces. Every one of these is already denied by `*` and none of them
# is under an allowed directory, so these lines change nothing today. They are
# here as a statement of intent that survives someone loosening a rule above:
# whatever else this build admits, it never admits these.
.git
.git/**
knowledge
knowledge/**
.cache
.env
.env.*
**/*.age
**/.DS_Store
